Laravel 12 and 13. @csrf + the web group's CSRF middleware (ValidateCsrfToken in 12, PreventRequestForgery in 13) give CSRF: a missing or wrong token is answered 419, and Laravel 13 also lets a browser request with Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin through without a token; throttle:contact gives 429; the honeypot is a visually hidden text input; delivery is a webhook POST with an 8s timeout and ->throw(), so any failure becomes a 502 and success is shown only after delivery. The line that sends the message is the only part the project replaces (Mail::to(...)->send(new ContactMessage($data)) also throws on transport failure and fits the same try/catch). Behind a proxy configure trusted proxies so $request->ip() is the visitor, not the proxy. A 502 uses resources/views/errors/5xx.blade.php (error-pages recipe).
