Laravel 12 and 13. The built-in `health: '/up'` route only answers 200 when the app booted (500 otherwise) and does not touch the database; use this route as the monitored endpoint, or throw from a DiagnosingHealth listener to make /up fail. Routes registered in the `then:` closure get no middleware group, so no session or cookie is started: with the default database session driver, a /healthz inside routes/web.php would fail with 500 instead of 503 when the DB is down. routes/stateless.php is shared with the robots-sitemap recipe: keep both route sets in that one file. The bootstrap/app.php blocks of the headers, health-endpoint, robots-sitemap and error-pages recipes are parts of one ->withRouting/->withMiddleware/->withExceptions chain: merge them, each call once. Keep the DB connect timeout short in config/database.php (connection 'options').
